    "plan_strategy": "Move the ranking axis first by switching from the linear baseline to a gradient-boosted tree ensemble (hist_gbdt), which on adult-style tabular data with 102 one-hot columns and strong non-linear/interaction structure (education-num x marital-status x capital-gain) reliably lifts roc_auc/KS well beyond the 0.6496 the bar needs. Simultaneously set the operating point with class_weight='balanced' so the default predict() rule cuts near the balanced-accuracy optimum instead of at the majority-favouring 0.5, since recall (0.6008) vs specificity is where the baseline lost most of its balanced accuracy. Configuration: hist_gbdt, max_iter=400 with learning_rate=0.06, max_leaf_nodes=31, min_samples_leaf=20, l2_regularization=1.0, early_stopping=True with validation_fraction=0.1 and n_iter_no_change=25 so the iteration count self-limits inside the 600s budget. Preprocessing: impute='none' so the trees split on NaN in workclass/occupation/native-country directly (no missing_indicator, which is bit-for-bit redundant under impute:none), and scale=False since trees are scale-invariant. Then read cut_headroom, recall vs specificity, and balanced_accuracy_at_best_cut on the validation split: if cut_headroom is small, the remaining gap is ranking and attempt 2 goes to a different ensemble family or deeper trees; if cut_headroom is large, attempt 2 tunes an explicit class weight map instead of 'balanced'."
